From: tezuka_fw
Subject: [PATCH] wg-quick: pause after setconf before address add

The "ip address add" call that immediately follows "wg setconf" in
cmd_up() can lose a race against the newly-configured WireGuard
interface settling in the kernel's routing/FIB state on some
platforms. Observed reliably on a Zynq-7010 ARM SoC (PlutoPlus):
"RTNETLINK answers: Network is unreachable" on every automated
wg-quick run, while the identical ip/wg commands succeed every time
when spaced apart manually (even a 1s gap is enough). Retrying the
whole wg-quick invocation does not help, since each attempt reproduces
the same zero-delay setconf-then-addr-add sequence internally.

See github.com/mbehn1976/tezuka_fw pull/4 for the on-hardware
diagnosis this patch is based on.

--- a/src/wg-quick/linux.bash
+++ b/src/wg-quick/linux.bash
@@ -330,6 +330,13 @@ cmd_up() {
 	add_if
 	execute_hooks "${PRE_UP[@]}"
 	set_config
+	# The immediately-following "ip address add" can lose a race against
+	# the newly-configured WireGuard interface settling in the kernel's
+	# routing/FIB state on some platforms (observed reliably on a Zynq-7010
+	# ARM SoC: "RTNETLINK answers: Network is unreachable"). A brief pause
+	# here avoids it; the same commands succeed every time when spaced
+	# apart manually. See github.com/mbehn1976/tezuka_fw pull/4.
+	sleep 1
 	for i in "${ADDRESSES[@]}"; do
 		add_addr "$i"
 	done
